es-rgds-panelguis: VIEW GAME MEDIA, the save state manager and manual scrape on the bottom panel, sized to fit it

The dual-screen themes run ES on a canvas three panels wide (1920x480 on the RG DS, 3072x768 on the Plus), and
es-rgds-uiwidth.patch sizes the menus against one panel (ES_UI_WIDTH), centred on the bottom one. Three screens were
still sized against the whole canvas:

- VIEW GAME MEDIA (GuiImageViewer), its zoom view and VIEW FULLSCREEN VIDEO (GuiVideoViewer) took all three panels. A
  picture was fitted to the canvas' height only, so a wide one ran over the top screen and off the bottom one, and the
  zoom view (centred on the top panel) was zoomed to the canvas' width (ROCKNIXDS issue 32). They now take the bottom
  panel. The zoom view opens with the whole picture fitted to the panel (L/R zoom from there), and a zoomed or moved
  picture stays on the panel. VIEW GAME MEDIA's zoom view was also always empty: it got the entry's number ("1")
  instead of its picture.
- The save state manager (GuiSaveState) was as wide as the canvas: its title sat on the bottom screen, START NEW GAME
  on the top one and the last third of the tiles off the screen (ROCKNIXDS issue 34). It is one panel wide now, on the
  bottom one, with the 4.5 tiles of a 4:3 screen (13.6 across the canvas).
- Manual scrape (ScraperSearchComponent): in a window one panel wide (576 or 922 px) the labels took nearly all of the
  metadata's 24%, so a publisher, developer or genre showed a letter or two and the release date ran out of the window
  (ROCKNIXDS issue 35). The values now get as much room as the labels, and at least what a date and the rating stars
  take, from the result list's share (at most 45% of the window). A label that doesn't fit is shortened with "...",
  never the date. The column only grows: where 24% is enough (a big screen), the layout is stock.
- A message box with an icon (GuiMsgBox) measured its text at the box's whole width, though the icon's column takes
  part of it: a message of several lines (the notice after a game that stopped responding) wrapped once more than the
  box was made for, and its first and last lines ran over the box's edges. The text is measured at the width it gets.

